issues.body:
With default options, `processEntities` is `true` and the five predefined XML entities (`&lt;`, `&gt;`, `&amp;`, `&quot;`, `&apos;`) are decoded as expected. Numeric character references, however, are silently left as literal text unless `htmlEntities: true` is passed:

```js
const { XMLParser } = require('fast-xml-parser');

const parser = new XMLParser(); // processEntities defaults to true

parser.parse('<r>&lt;&gt;&amp;</r>');
// { r: '<>&' }  -- named entities decoded

parser.parse('<r>&#65;&#x42;</r>');
// { r: '&#65;&#x42;' }  -- numeric refs NOT decoded (expected: { r: 'AB' })

parser.parse('<r a="&#65;"/>', { ignoreAttributes: false });
// attributes affected the same way

new XMLParser({ htmlEntities: true }).parse('<r>&#65;&#x42;</r>');
// { r: 'AB' }  -- only decoded when htmlEntities is on
```

Numeric character references are not HTML entities - they are part of core XML. Per the XML 1.0 spec (section 4.1, "Character and Entity References"), a well-formed XML processor must recognize `&#...;` and `&#x...;` references in content and attribute values, independent of any HTML entity table.

Looking at the source, this seems to come from `OrderedObjParser` wiring `numericAllowed` to the `htmlEntities` option when constructing the `EntityDecoder`:

```js
this.entityDecoder = new EntityDecoder({
  namedEntities: { ...namedEntities, ...externalEntities },
  numericAllowed: this.options.htmlEntities, // <-- ties XML char refs to the HTML entities option
  ...
});
```

So a user who parses XML with default options (and `htmlEntities` defaults to `false`) gets raw `&#65;` sequences in their data, while named XML entities are decoded. I ran into this with a feed that encodes non-ASCII characters as numeric refs.

Tested on fast-xml-parser 5.11.2 (npm latest) with Node 22. The same behavior exists in 4.x, so it may be intentional - but if so, it would be great to have it documented next to `processEntities`, since the current default contradicts XML spec compliance.

Currently I can have the parser create a text node on tags without attributes by setting `alwaysCreateTextNode: true`. However, for self closing tags, I would like the parser to not create a text node. Right now it creates a text node set to an empty string. 

I also noticed that if a self closing tags have any attributes, a text node is not created. It only seems like it's created when a self closing tags have no attributes.

```ts
// Given these settings:
const parser = new XMLParser({
	ignoreAttributes: false,
	allowBooleanAttributes: true,
	parseTagValue: false,
	alwaysCreateTextNode: true,
});

parser.parse("<workbookPr defaultThemeVersion="153222" />") 
// "workbookPr": {
//   "@_defaultThemeVersion": "153222"
// }

parser.parse("<workbookPr />") 
// "workbookPr": {
//   "#text": ""
// }
```

I expect that `"#text"` is always present, no matter if the self closing tag have attributes or not. But my primary expectation is to be able to have no text nodes at all for self closing tags while still allowing the parser to make text nodes for tags without any attributes.
